Recall that $\dk=\tau^{i_k}\depsilon$ for some integer $i_k$ so that if $\dk>\depsilon$, then $\dk\geq \tau^{-1}\depsilon$, whence $\dkun\geq \tau\dk\geq \depsilon$. Next, assume that $\dk\leq \depsilon$, and observe that $\normii{\nabla f(\xk)}>\eps$ since $\Tepsilon>k$. If $\iak\ijk=1$, it follows from Lemma~\ref{smallLemmaHaar} that the iteration is successful. Consequently, $\dkun=\min\accolade{\tau^{-1}\dk,\dmax}$. But if $\iak\ijk=0$, then it always holds that $\dkun\geq \tau\dk$. The proof is completed by observing that $\prob{\iak\ijk=1|\falgebra}=q$ for some $q\geq\tilde{\beta}$.
\end{proof}

